  "strategy": "1.Approach and rationale\n   We adopt a conditional Fourier analysis approach combined with non-convex optimization. The central idea is to represent the step function h(x) as a time- frequency representation of its autocorrelation function. This dual representation allows us to capture both the temporal structure (governed by the integral constraint) and the spectral properties (governing the overlap) of the function. \n\n   We assume h(x) can be well approximated by a periodic function with harmonics that balance the integral constraint and the overlap minimization. The conditional Fourier analysis first identifies the most significant frequency components, then uses these as constraints in a non-convex optimization problem. This differs from previous Local Search methods by explicitly targeting the global structure of the solution.\n\n   We will:\n   - Perform a spectral analysis of the parent solution to identify the dominant modes\n   - Use these modes as frequency filters for population-based searches\n   - Apply a phase-corrected optimization to maintain solution feasibility\n\n   This method bypasses the local optimal trap by:\n   - Treating frequency components as constraints\n   - Using Hilbert transform to enforce reality conditions\n   - Maintaining exponential stability criteria\n\n   The main limitation is that high-frequency modes might be insufficiently penalized, but our phase-correction strategy mitigates this.\n\n2.Mathematical specification\n\n   Representation:\n     Let n = 250 (default), then define frequency resolution \n     \u03a9 = 2\u03c0/n (dx factor)\n     Base Fourier basis: e^{i * l * \u03a9 * x} for l in [-L,L] (L is dynamic)\n\n   Variables:\n     Complex coefficients c_l for l \u2208 [-L,L]\n\n   Objectives:\n     C_5 = max_{k} ||h * (1-h)_{shifted}||_1 (with proper normalization)\n\n   Constraints:\n     Integral(const h)=1 \u2192 \u2211_{m} (iterations) integral(k_m)=1/2 (normalized)\n     Non-negativity: Re(c_l) >= -\u03b4, Im(c_l) >= -\u03b4 for appropriate \u03b4\n\n   Special formulations:\n     Use Ansatz: h(t) = \u03a3_{l\u2208S} c_l e^{i\u03c9_l t} + \u03a3_{l\u2208T} d_l (1 - e^{i\u03c9_l t})/2i\n     Then enforce Re(h(t)) \u2208 [0,1] via Hilbert transform\n\n   Derivation:\n     The Hilbert transform pairs positive and negative frequencies appropriately.\n\n3.Implementation procedure\n\n   Step 1: Preprocessing\n     - Verify inputs (seed, budget)\n     - Load initial_h_values if available (n=300)\n     - Set default n=150 if parent not available\n     - Compute k\u226410 frequency modes via PCA of autocorrelation\n\n   Step 2: Pre-filtering\n     - Identify dominant frequencies from parent solution (or random if not available)\n     - Keep only top M=4 modes (or less if dimension collapsing)\n     - Project parent solution into frequency space\n\n   Step 3: Dual constrained optimization\n     - Use WCGH variant with periodic conditioning (n mode changes)\n     - For each frequency combination (subset of top M modes), enforce:\n         \u222b h dx =1 \n         h: [0,2] \u2208 [0,1]\n         via penalty method\n\n   Step 4: Hilbert transform enforcements\n     - Apply weekly transform to stabilize frequency pairs\n     - Add corrective term to filter invalid phases\n\n   Step 5: Iterative refinement\n     - Break by 1000 function evaluations or time\n     - Global update step every 100 evaluations\n     - Use antithetic sampling for gradient calculation\n\n4.Search choices and variations\n\n   Default configuration:\n     n = 150-300 (determined by parent availability)\n     Frequency window: [-5,5] (signature frequency components)\n     Population size: min(20, 4 + log(error))\n     Integral penalty: 1e-4 * (actual-sum)^2\n     Weighted objective: 0.8*laplace_term + 0.01*overshoot\n\n   Adaptive controls:\n     If excessive oscillation:\n        Clamp frequency range to [0,3]\n        Increase weight of integral constraint\n        Apply damping factor 0.9\n\n     If slow convergence:\n        Increase population diversity by 30%\n        Add artificial noise in [0,0.001] range\n        Use directed crossover with learned weights\n\n5.Compute budget and robustness\n\n   Time allocation:\n     - 200s: Initial exploration\n     - 400s: Main optimization\n     - 500s: Local refinement\n\n   Hardware limits:\n     Max n=1000 (solution space size constrained)\n     Graceful degradation:\n        If time low: Use mode subset of size 3\n        If integral diverges: Switch to direct gradient projection\n        If ranks singular: Trigger re-sampling\n\n6.Validation and return contract\n\n   Feasibility check:\n     np.all( (h>=0) & (h<=1) )\n     abs(np.sum(h)-n/2) < 1e-2\n\n   Objective calculation:\n     corr = np.correlate(h, (1-h), mode='full')\n     c5 = (max(corr) * dx)  # dx=2.0/n\n\n   Acceptance criteria:\n     c5 <= target (0.3808)\n     Without parent: c5 <= 0.39\n   Return format:\n     (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) \n\n   Special handling:\n     If no valid solution: Return parent solution (if available) at configurable threshold.\n     If time is under 500s: Return best known candidate.",
